# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files with CRLF
# on checkout. For most of this repository that is cosmetic. For the two shell scripts it
# is fatal: bash reads the trailing carriage return as part of the interpreter name and
# fails before running a single line of either script -
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# or, further in, the equally unhelpful
#
#     $'\r': command not found
#
# Pinning them to LF costs nothing on Linux or macOS and is the difference between
# working and not under Git Bash.

# Normalise text files to LF inside the repository, and check them out with whatever the
# platform expects, unless a rule below overrides it.
* text=auto

# These must be LF on disk on every platform, for the reason above. `scripts/*.sh` are
# the ones that matter today; the glob covers any added later without anyone having to
# remember this file exists.
*.sh text eol=lf
